## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates the ability to design scalable, resilient multi-region systems with per-tenant isolation, hot-tenant mitigation, and cost control—core competencies for distributed systems engineers at senior levels.

## Key Concepts

- Time-based shard design and tenant isolation strategies
- Multi-region replication with quorum-based consistency
- Hot-tenant mitigation and dynamic shard rebalancing
- Data lifecycle management: tiered storage, retention, compaction, and archival

## Code Example

```javascript
function shardKey(tenantId, timestamp) {
  const hourBucket = Math.floor(timestamp / 3600000);
  return `${tenantId}:${hourBucket}`;
}

class TimeSeriesStore {
  constructor(regions, replicationFactor = 3) {
    this.regions = regions;
    this.replicationFactor = replicationFactor;
    this.quorum = Math.floor(replicationFactor / 2) + 1;
  }

  async write(tenantId, timestamp, data) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const writes = replicas.map(region => this.writeToRegion(region, key, data));
    await Promise.all(writes);
    return this.verifyQuorum(writes);
  }

  selectReplicas(shardKey) {
    return this.regions.slice(0, this.replicationFactor);
  }

  async read(tenantId, timestamp) {
    const key = shardKey(tenantId, timestamp);
    const replicas = this.selectReplicas(key);
    const reads = replicas.map(region => this.readFromRegion(region, key));
    return this.mergeResults(await Promise.all(reads));
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question evaluates cache invalidation strategies at scale, per-tenant data isolation, and stampede protection mechanisms in Redis-based systems. It also tests understanding of Pub/Sub invalidation patterns and lightweight locking approaches.

## Key Concepts

- Write-through cache architecture
- Per-tenant TTL management and version stamping
- Distributed locking to prevent cache stampede
- Pub/Sub-based invalidation and out-of-band refresh
- Performance trade-offs: data freshness vs. system load

## Code Example

```javascript
async function getProduct(id, tenant) {
  const cacheKey = `prod:${tenant}:${id}`;
  let data = await redis.get(cacheKey);
  if (data) return JSON.parse(data);

  const lockKey = `${cacheKey}:lock`;
  const lock = await redis.set(lockKey, '1', 'PX', 5000, 'NX');

  if (lock) {
    try {
      const freshData = await fetchFromDB(id, tenant);
      await redis.setex(cacheKey, 300, JSON.stringify(freshData));
      return freshData;
    } finally {
      await redis.del(lockKey);
    }
  }

  await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 100));
  return getProduct(id, tenant);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design robust cache invalidation with multiple caches, versioned keys, and a practical flow that avoids stampede.

## Key Concepts
- Cache-aside pattern with per-key versioning
- TTL-based data keys and version keys in Redis
- Fallback to Memcached and consistency between layers
- SWR to prevent repeated DB hits during refresh

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal get with versioned cache
async function getProduct(id) {
  let ver = await redis.get(`product:ver:${id}`);
  if (!ver) ver = '1';
  let data = await redis.get(`product:data:${id}:${ver}`);
  if (data) return JSON.parse(data);
  const row = await db.query('SELECT * FROM products WHERE id=?', [id]);
  await redis.set(`product:data:${id}:${ver}`, JSON.stringify(row), 'EX', 60);
  return row;
}

// Minimal update with invalidation
async function updateProduct(id, updates) {
  await db.run('UPDATE products SET ... WHERE id=?', [id]);
  await redis.incr(`product:ver:${id}`); // invalidates current cache
}
```

